{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Upstream search result — the defect is real, known, and unfixed (checked 2026-10-27 ~22:10 PDT)\n\nSearched before proposing any local fix, per the rule that \"the wheel is broken\" is a claim about someone else's software.\n\n**Upstream issue: [JessicaTegner/pypandoc#447](https://github.com/JessicaTegner/pypandoc/issues/447) — \"Prebuild wheel for macos pretends to be arm, while it's a intel binary\"**, opened 2026-08-11 by RemiDesgrange. It reports exactly this ticket's measurement on the same version:\n\n```\nTag: py3-none-macosx_11_0_arm64\nlipo -archs .venv/.../pypandoc/files/pandoc  ->  x86_64\n```\n\nand names the same reason it has become visible now: \"New macOS build (since Tahoe?) will not have Rosetta include.\"\n\nState of the issue as of this check: **open, no assignee, no labels, no linked branch or pull request, no milestone, and no maintainer reply in the activity log.** So there is no upstream fix to wait for and no upstream-suggested workaround to adopt.\n\n**No newer release exists.** The PyPI simple index for `pypandoc-binary` lists macOS arm64 wheels for 1.13, 1.14, 1.15, 1.16, 1.16.2 and 1.17; **1.17 is the newest**. Upgrading cannot resolve this, and there is no post-1.17 build carrying a native binary.\n\n### What this settles for the proposed options\n\n- Waiting for upstream is not an option: the issue is 2.5 months old with no owner and no PR.\n- Pinning a different `pypandoc_binary` version is not an option: no release ships a native arm64 pandoc.\n- Option 2 (plain `pypandoc` + a host pandoc) remains viable but shifts setup onto every contributor and CI.\n- **Option 1 stays the recommendation** — verify the bundled binary's architecture at bootstrap and repair it from the pinned upstream pandoc release when it does not match `uname -m`. It is portable (no CI-provider trigger), keeps the existing `pypandoc==1.17` / `pandoc 3.9` pins that `test_pypandoc_and_pandoc_versions_are_pinned` asserts, and self-heals every new worktree rather than requiring the manual per-venv copy.\n\nThis matches the shape already decided for the sibling host-pin bug `corking-phonolite-drafthorse` (`0461-3c72-9e38-4ce5`): fetch the required release into a version-keyed, checksum-verified cache under the repo's own cache directory, verify the download before use, and never modify what is on `PATH` or inside another tool's install. The two bugs are the same class — a pinned artifact that is not what its pin claims — and should share one bootstrap-repair mechanism rather than growing two, which also keeps the mechanism-delta ratchet satisfied.\n\n### Still unverified\n\nWhether the x86_64 binary is present in the Linux/Windows wheels' arm64 variants, or only in the macOS arm64 wheel. CI runs `ubuntu-latest` x86_64 and is unaffected either way, so this was not measured. No local fix was implemented in this session; the manual per-venv copy remains the only thing keeping `make verify` green on this host."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793163629943638104,"uuid":"6b7dfb16-9848-4191-9527-f610b0fbd868"}